DEPLOY
------

# dokimion_common.conf replaces the old per-host site file dokimion1.conf.
#
# nginx reads sites *through* sites-enabled/*.conf and treats a BROKEN SYMLINK
# there as fatal. Do not simply rename dokimion1.conf inside sites-available --
# that leaves sites-enabled/dokimion1.conf dangling and nginx will not start.
# Disable the old site in sites-enabled instead, and leave the file itself in
# sites-available so rollback needs no repo access.
#
# Check what is actually enabled first:
ls -l /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

# dokimion_common.conf is stored ONCE in the repo, under dokimion1, and is shared
# by all three production and all three staging web servers. The four .h files are
# this host's own; every include in dokimion_common.conf resolves against
# sites-available, so they all land there.
#
# ==> BECAUSE IT IS SHARED, EDITING IT REACHES STAGING TOO. <==
# Nothing about this directory's name suggests staging depends on it, but s-dokimion{1,2,3}
# install the same file. Keep anything environment-specific in the .h includes. The same
# applies to production/dokimion/load_balancer.conf, shared by both load balancers.
R=~/dokimion/config/production
sudo install -m 644 -t /etc/nginx/sites-available \
  $R/dokimion1/dokimion_common.conf \
  $R/dokimion1/server_name.h $R/dokimion1/webserver_cert.h \
  $R/dokimion1/lb_access.h   $R/dokimion1/lb_mtls.h

# VERIFY THE INSTALL LANDED, BEFORE RELOADING. A failed `install` leaves the previous
# valid config in place, so `nginx -t` passes and the reload succeeds while nothing has
# changed -- no warning, every signal healthy. On these boxes `install` is
# coreutils-from-uutils, whose "No such file or directory" does not name the missing path,
# so it reads like a missing program. This no-op cost 3.5 hours on dokimion3 during the
# mTLS rollout (mtls_h1_deploy.md) and six days of live wildcard CORS on s-dokimion1
# (security_hardening_staging.md, M1 + L4). Because this file is shared by six servers, a
# single source copy is NOT evidence that any given server received it -- check all six.
md5sum /etc/nginx/sites-available/dokimion_common.conf \
       $R/dokimion1/dokimion_common.conf   # must match

# security_hardening H1 — lb_access.h and lb_mtls.h are required includes:
# dokimion_common.conf includes both, and nginx refuses to start if either file is
# missing. Both ship inert on production (lb_access.h is "allow all"), so behaviour
# is unchanged until the restriction is deliberately enabled. webserver_cert.h is
# required too -- it carries the :443 certificate paths.

# Swap the enabled site.
sudo ln -sfn ../sites-available/dokimion_common.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dokimion_common.conf
sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dokimion1.conf

sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx

# ROLLBACK — dokimion1.conf is still in sites-available, so this needs no repo:
#   sudo ln -sfn ../sites-available/dokimion1.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dokimion1.conf
#   sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dokimion_common.conf
#   sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx
# This README previously said `mv dokimion1.conf dokimion1.conf_good`. If that rename
# was ever run here, the retired file is dokimion1.conf_good -- check
# `ls /etc/nginx/sites-available/` and use that name, or the symlink will dangle.
